I'm going to give I'm going to

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:26.840
go into the one of my case
studies. Now. So I'm in the end

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:30.290
of the 1990s. And and I just
want to say that I decided to,

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:32.390
it's really hard to know when
you write a book, and you want

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:34.700
to talk about everything, and
you can, you know, you don't

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:38.150
really know what to decide to
focus on, I really wanted to

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:41.330
focus on the standardization of
web metrics, which is the end of

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:44.210
the 1990s and the beginning of
2000s. Because I think that

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:49.130
everything that we experienced
today, around profiling around

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:52.430
fake news around disinformation
and misinformation, and

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:57.320
basically, the problematic and
broken online ecosystem where we

00:16:57.350 --> 00:17:01.850
are the product started in those
times. So I think a lot of the

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:05.150
times, you know, people talk
only about the last decade, but

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:09.260
we're talking about processes
that happened probably 20 years

00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:12.200
and probably even before, but I
think it's really important to

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:16.730
identify key moments where these
kind of things, which is

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:19.820
basically the surveillance of
our online behavior have become

00:17:19.820 --> 00:17:25.520
normalized. And so in this case
study, I'm basically looking at

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:29.870
how did the standardization of
web metrics, which means how to

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:33.380
measure different kind of
behaviors in order to trade them

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:38.420
in an efficient way. So as you
can see here, of IAB, which is

00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:42.110
the Interactive Advertising
Bureau, I wanted to standardize

00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:44.720
different kind of metrics.
Because in those times, if you

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:49.160
remember, the internet wasn't
really, the web wasn't really a

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:53.240
technology that which was a sort
of that people knew that it's

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:56.240
going to succeed. So a lot of
the time we take for granted

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:59.000
that the internet is succeeding,
but actually, in those times,

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:02.630
that the kind of subscription
model didn't quite work. And

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:05.630
with the dotcom, bubble crash,
people didn't really know what

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:08.420
to do. And therefore different
kind of company specific

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:11.780
companies managed to survive
the.com bubble crash, including

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:14.990
Amazon, and then different kind
of platforms started to emerge,

00:18:15.170 --> 00:18:18.710
which gave a free service.
Whereas, you know, as we all

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:21.290
know, it wasn't quite free, but
we were the product. So let's

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:27.620
see what happened in those days.
So this kind of the IB wanted to

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:30.650
actually understand how can we
standardize different kind of

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:34.400
measuring units? How can we
standardize how we measure

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:37.850
people's behavior in order to
make this kind of product? which

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:41.810
is our behavior as efficient as
possible? Because if we are the

00:18:41.810 --> 00:18:45.950
product, then the kind of the
currency needs to be agreed upon

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:49.490
with all of the actors that are
involved. Does you can see here,

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:52.490
these were the most sort of
common

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:55.580
metrics.

00:18:57.230 --> 00:19:00.350
And what they basically wanted
to understand is, how are they

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:04.040
going to measure it through
different kind of angles? Will

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:06.710
it be through the ad serving?
Will it be through people's

00:19:06.710 --> 00:19:10.760
computer, and what will actually
count as a click what will count

00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:14.930
as a total visit, and what will
count as an ad impression? Now,

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:17.240
for me, one of the most
interesting thing that was part

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:22.010
of this research was to actually
analyze different kind of

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:25.160
internet standards through the
IETF, which is the Internet

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:29.330
Engineering Task Force. And
also, I analyze different kind

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:31.970
of legal documents in order to
understand how do they actually

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:36.830
decide what's happening there.
So when I was starting to read

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:40.700
the cookie standard, which is
what you're looking at here, I

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:43.580
started to realize that
actually, what's happening in

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:48.560
the backend is that when you
visit a website, you through

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:52.460
different kinds of default
settings, you are being sent a

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:55.220
lot of cookies, which could be
dozens of cookies could be

00:19:55.220 --> 00:19:59.600
hundreds of cookies, in order to
basically send different kind of

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:02.690
information. About your
behavior, if it's from the

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:05.450
website that you type on, that
would be first party cookies. So

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:08.180
that means that, you know, if,
for example, I look at The

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:11.690
Guardian, then it will be a
first party cookie that the

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:16.040
Guardian collects information on
me. But if it's third party

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:18.740
cookies, it could be ad
exchanges, or different kind of

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:24.110
data brokers, who are also gonna
sort of listen to my behavior

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:27.800
across different kind of
platforms, and throughout time.

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:32.120
So what I basically saw here is
that there is a standard, and

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:35.390
we're actually not really aware
of what's happening. What was

00:20:35.390 --> 00:20:38.450
really interesting with the
standard, is that the people who

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:41.210
suggested it at the beginning
said, Well, actually, maybe we

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:44.960
will show part of the standard
will be to show people what's

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:46.880
happening in the back end,
because people don't really

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:50.780
understand that all of these
these things are happening. And

00:20:50.780 --> 00:20:53.630
advertising industry at that
time said, No, no, it's going to

00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:56.900
confuse people. So let's just
show them, you know, that the

00:20:56.900 --> 00:21:00.380
front end, as computer
scientists call it, so that it

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:02.720
wouldn't confuse them to see
what's happening in the back

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:07.970
end. Um, but actually, when we
think about cookies, and one of

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:10.070
the things that struck me is
that all of the people who are

00:21:10.070 --> 00:21:12.950
talking about cookies, whether
it's computer scientists, or

00:21:12.950 --> 00:21:17.840
even media scholars, because
just a text file, but this just

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:21.620
a text file is actually a
communication form, is what

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:25.910
cookies actually do, is to print
different kind of text files.

00:21:25.910 --> 00:21:28.910
But every time that you make
these kind of whether I'm

00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:32.210
reading an article, or I'm
clicking on things, these kind

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:36.050
of things are communicated to
different kind of entities. And

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:39.200
those can be either, as I said,
before the Guardian if it's

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:42.860
first party cookies, or it could
be other data brokers, or

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:46.430
different kind of agencies,
which we are not aware of. So

00:21:46.430 --> 00:21:51.170
this is what I was talking about
sort of knowledge not going into

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:54.560
the common sense, or the common
way of defining different

00:21:54.560 --> 00:21:58.010
things. This is one of these
moments where I realized that

00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:01.040
actually, you know, we've been
sold that cookies are just text

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:05.990
file, this is also part of a
legislation that's going on

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:09.860
behind it. But actually, it's a
form of communication, because

00:22:09.860 --> 00:22:12.710
they're communicating different
kinds of topics, which could be

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:17.300
my gender, the kind of device
that I'm using, the kind of a

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:19.700
broadband that I'm using, and
different kinds of things

00:22:19.880 --> 00:22:21.140
throughout time.

00:22:22.550 --> 00:22:26.840
So one of the other things that
the advertising industry wanted

00:22:26.840 --> 00:22:31.220
to do was because there was all
of these kind of robotic

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:34.910
behavior, they wanted to
understand who is human and who

00:22:34.910 --> 00:22:38.090
is not human, because it was
really important for them, if

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:41.150
we're the product, it's
important to have exact

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:44.300
measurements. So what they did
was to develop this different

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:48.290
kind of made measurements, and
what they call filtration, and

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:52.310
filter, who's human, and who is
not human in order to make

00:22:52.430 --> 00:22:56.540
accurate measurements of what is
happening so that they can treat

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:59.480
us. So as you can see here, they
develop different guides. So for

00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:03.200
example, the base basic method,
which was a text file of robots,

00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:06.770
the other one was identification
of specific suspicious or non

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:10.460
human activity, which was a
different kind of lists that you

00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:14.600
were supposed to send the IAB.
And another thing was to analyze

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:18.110
the rhythms of users activity,
which I found really

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:21.980
interesting, because what they
were basically defining there is

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:26.150
what are the kind of behaviors
that are can be considered as

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:29.210
your bodek? And hence non human?
And what are the kind of

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:33.710
behaviors that can be defined as
humans? And as you can see here,

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:38.480
these are the kind of
definitions that they had of

00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:42.860
what is the robotic behavior,
the sort of what did they

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:46.820
analyze in order to establish
that, so they establish what

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:50.450
users performing multiple
sequential activities, users

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:54.470
with highest levels of
activities, users who acted in

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:58.160
consistent interaction
attributes, and other suspicious

00:23:58.190 --> 00:24:02.480
activities. And so what they
would do with that is basically

00:24:02.570 --> 00:24:06.500
not only to try to understand
and to measure people, but also

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:10.490
to define what is a human and
non human behavior. And for me,

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:14.480
actually, one of the first times
that I realized that was in my

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:18.320
first book, when I published it,
all of the people that I

00:24:18.320 --> 00:24:22.340
